On Tue, 15 Jan 2007, 777 wrote:
>
> I have a question about the method in which the record file is updated
> and displayed when the toptenwin option is set on.
> Specifically, why is the scoreboard displayed to the user while it is
> still be written back into the record file?

   I haven't studied the code closely, but here are two alternatives I
can think of, with their drawbacks:

     * Scan through the file and insert the new score, remembering
       where you put it.
     * Reopen the file and print the top N and the M around the new score.

Vulnerability: May not print the new score if a lot of games are
finished between step 1 and step 2, pushing the new score down.

     * Scan through the file and insert the new score, remembering
       the top N and the M around it.
     * Print those N+M scores.

Disadvantage: Requires a buffer in memory big enough for N+M score
records, which might be a lot by NetHack standards (but probably not).

   Since IMHO this is definitely a serious bug, I'd say go ahead and
fix it with the second alternative above, and hang the memory expense! :)
Or if you have a better idea, do that.

   I don't see this bug on the official known-bugs list yet, BTW.

